 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "POSTBRIDGE CLAPPER BRIDGE. Three huge granite slabs on stone piers, about 700 years old. 'Clapper' comes from an old word for a stepping place. It still holds."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "HOUND TOR. A heap of granite that in fog looks like a pack of hounds frozen mid-howl. Conan Doyle walked here before writing 'The Hound of the Baskervilles'. Below lie the ruins of a medieval village."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "WISTMAN'S WOOD. Tiny twisted oaks wrapped in moss, growing between boulders. Said to be the haunt of the Wild Hunt. Ghostly hounds, bad weather and a sense of humour."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "DARTMOOR NATIONAL PARK. 954 square km of open moor, the largest wild stretch in southern England. Sheep, ponies, tors and weather. The weather is the star."
  },
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "BODMIN MOOR AND TINTAGEL, WEST. Mind the fog. There's a rumour of a big cat. There's definitely a big sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "dartmoor_prison",
   "title": "Dartmoor Prison",
   "text": "At Princetown stands Dartmoor Prison, built between 1806 and 1809 to hold French prisoners of the Napoleonic Wars, and later Americans from the War of 1812. It became an ordinary prison in 1850. Escaping is not advised: the moor has beaten tougher walkers."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "dartmoor_letterboxing",
   "title": "Letterboxing",
   "text": "In 1854 a Dartmoor guide, James Perrott, left a bottle at remote Cranmere Pool for walkers to leave their cards in. It grew into letterboxing: boxes hidden across the moor, each with a rubber stamp and a logbook. Somewhere out there, one is waiting for you."
  }
 ],
